00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:23.880
So I'm measuring this,

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:27.060
whether it be percent
dissolution or some kind of

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:29.160
quality metric that happens at
the end of the manufacturing

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:31.200
process in that
product, in that site.

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:33.720
So for that particular
combination,

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:37.080
what's the historical data look
like and what are the control

00:26:37.080 --> 00:26:39.120
limits that I want to set.
And there's all kinds of rules,

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:41.820
all the [INAUDIBLE] heart
control chart rules,

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:46.500
something bell, electric
bell-- what was it, Kelly?

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:49.980
I can't remember all the names of
the eight roles there you can select from.

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:53.640
And so there's a module
to then select the

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.240
control chart limits.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:26:59.220
And that'll only
be given to a few--

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:02.520
the access will be given to
a few of our manufacturing colleagues.

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:05.880
So that's pretty powerful to be
able to set those control limits.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:09.240
And then the more general
module will be to have that

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:12.840
continuous monitoring of those
and another module that allows

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:15.840
you to go in and actually
make annotations so you can type in,

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:19.380
OK, this particular data
point is out of spec,

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:21.180
here's the reason why.
And so that gets tracked.

00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:24.120
Anyone can go back and see that
of why it went out of control

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:28.560
as well as things
like email alerting.

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:28.980
So

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:33.720
your process is drifting out
of control, it hit Control limit.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:36.420
Now you get an email
alert right to your inbox.

00:27:36.420 --> 00:27:38.340
And so then you can
quickly go and investigate.

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:42.240
So this is something
that hasn't been widely

00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:45.120
utilized throughout
the company.

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:48.060
There's definitely control
starting in different sites,

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:51.960
but not a universal tool
that's been built to solve

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:54.060
this controlled charting

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.340
challenge across the board.

00:27:56.340 --> 00:27:57.900
And that's one of the

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:02.220
benefits of the way that
Catalina and team have designed

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:08.100
the database and has to be scalable.
And so it's not just a bespoke

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:11.040
solution for just one of
our major sites or two.

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:14.520
But now the way the data comes
in and the way they formatted it,

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:19.800
we're able to take the
solution and basically say, if

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:20.520
your data

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:26.160
is being loaded into SAP and
so forth or into our electronic

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:26.940
batch record system,

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:29.760
then you have it be able to
be exposed to you, you can

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:32.430
create controlled
charts, et cetera.

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:35.820
So for a couple of dozen
of our sites or so,

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:37.320
we're able to do
this type of work.

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:39.960
We're in the process
now of validating it

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:42.840
and having some
alpha users use it.

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:44.340
So we haven't fully
deployed it yet.

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:46.980
And our goal is
to then have it be

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:50.820
GMP certified so that we
can make those types of GMP

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:51.780
decisions based on that.

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:57.000
But we've made quite a bit
of progress in that space of doing

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:58.560
a continuous process
verification.

00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:00.420
So that's one.

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:02.400
And the next one--

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:04.920
I really should have Catalina
talk about it because she's the

00:29:04.920 --> 00:29:08.640
main data scientist that's
been doing it. So Catalina,

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:10.920
I don't know if you want
to come off mute and--

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:13.560
or if you're going to talk
about the monoclonal antibody,

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:14.580
but just kind of high level.

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:18.480
I'll try to do it justice,

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:19.980
but--

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:25.860
So one of our major products
that we manufacture is a

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:26.700
monoclonal antibody.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:31.800
And these are for different
products for dogs or cats.

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:35.880
One is Cytopoint which is
a product that is used for

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:38.340
basically itching,

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:42.360
dog's illness for
Librela and Solensia.

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:44.940
Those are for osteoarthritis

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:46.080
in dogs and cats.

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:49.920
And these are monoclonal
antibodies that need to be

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:52.080
manufactured as part of
those drug substances.

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:56.160
And those of you that are
involved in monoclonal antibody

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:57.600
manufacturing-- it's complex.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:00.120
There's a lot of
variables, it's

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:03.180
a difficult thing to

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:06.480
really get your
handle on. And so

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:12.900
one of the things that Catalina
and team have done is they

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:16.980
have built a Posit app
that takes a lot of these

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:21.120
real time-- I'll
call it real time--

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:22.800
but very close to real time

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:26.700
process data from the
batches that are being

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:28.920
actually scaled up and grown

00:30:30.300 --> 00:30:31.500
in our bioreactors

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:35.700
to be on our cloud platform
where things can get loaded and

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:40.440
deposit and then presented back to the
users as, I'll call it, near real time

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:44.760
visuals of what's going on with
the current batches as well as

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:46.560
some smart analytics.

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:49.260
So we have another data
scientist involved in this

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:52.920
that's from our team in Austria
who built some algorithms to help detect

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:56.460
when these very
complex multivariable

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:03.780
relationships show an out of control
signal and then send that signal to--

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:07.260
could be an email alert or just
signal it on the actual chart itself.

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:11.640
And this has been extremely
valuable. For one,

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:13.680
it's really hard
for these shop floor

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:17.880
engineers to be able to get access
to data because they might have to

00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:21.120
log into SAP, download
a spreadsheet,

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:23.820
or they may have to log
in to their MES system,

00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:26.400
download data to Excel, manually

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:31.140
manipulate it, which is
so cumbersome, whereas now,

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:33.780
we have it all flowing
automatically up into the cloud

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:36.060
platform which
then can be easily

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:39.000
and readily deployed through

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:43.020
tools like Posit to be able
to build these visuals and these

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:46.020
smart analytics on top of that
to be able to make better decisions.

00:31:48.480 --> 00:31:49.200
So

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:50.400
in the area of manufacturing,

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:52.440
that's the area that
we've actually just scratched the

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:56.580
surface. So the
monoclonal antibody interaction process

00:31:56.580 --> 00:32:00.360
has an upstream and a
downstream process. We've only just

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:03.540
tackle the upstream part of
one of those three products.

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.660
We want to do upstream
and downstream across all three

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:08.700
products and scale
across multiple sites.

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:09.720
So we've only done one site,

00:32:10.500 --> 00:32:11.880
upstream only, and
of one product.

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:15.060
So you can see we have a
lot more we can scale and a lot

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:16.140
more opportunity to

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:20.640
increase the value of these
tools house and Posit across

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:22.560
our manufacturing network.

00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:25.920
So that's a little bit more.

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:29.280
I can keep going. There's a
whole lot more things. But Robert,

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:31.800
is there any other questions I
should tackle or want me to

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:33.240
keep going on more use cases?

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:34.920
- Oh, there are a
lot of questions.

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:39.420
There is one that is
asked in the chat by

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:42.060
Ama. Sorry if I'm
mispronouncing your name.

00:32:42.060 --> 00:32:44.040
I don't know if you
want to ask it live.

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:50.220
I'll give you a few seconds.
But if not, I can ask it.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:51.240
I'll assume that

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:54.540
I'll ask it. Thanks for
sharing your experience--

