MAN: Hello?

00:33:07.355 --> 00:33:07.751
ROBERT BETHELL: Oh, come on in.

00:33:07.751 --> 00:33:07.815
MAN: Yeah, thanks.

00:33:07.815 --> 00:33:07.904
And thanks for having me.

00:33:07.904 --> 00:33:08.082
I just want to go straight
to the [INAUDIBLE] time.

00:33:08.081 --> 00:33:08.220
I know I'm based in
the UK in London and

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:11.400
I'm a student possibly working
on a startup with my co-founder.

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:14.340
And so was surprised about
that because it's a difficult startup.

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:17.040
And so I was asking
myself, we as students,

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:18.720
how you actually recommend

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:20.400
that's not, let me say,

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:23.580
a small practice-- like I
don't-- like I said, practice

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:28.920
[INAUDIBLE] getting the data
the data practice in-house with

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:31.740
a small team, just
basically three of us.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:34.860
And so that's what it was
like coming from because you work

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:36.810
with big companies. So
it's like one [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:38.100
institution, how
do I go about that?

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:41.400
- Yep, got it. And
I can see that--

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:42.120
thank you for that question.

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:44.760
I can see your questions
here typed up in the chat.

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:49.440
I did have a little bit of
that experience when I did a kind of

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:53.160
startup business analytics
group earlier in my career at

00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:54.840
Dow AgroSciences. Basically,

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.380
I came in and I was
given the headcount to hire three people.

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:01.620
So there were four of us.
So a small and mighty team,

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:03.960
really dedicated to help make

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:07.080
a difference using data and
analytics in our commercial space.

00:34:07.680 --> 00:34:09.660
And I learned a lot
from that. I mean,

00:34:09.660 --> 00:34:13.620
I learned a lot from mistakes
than actual successes.

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:15.060
So let me tell you
something of my mistakes

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:17.460
and then maybe that'll
help guide you.

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:21.060
And try to learn from those
mistakes as I've built this new

00:34:21.060 --> 00:34:22.080
team here at Zoetis.

00:34:22.740 --> 00:34:23.280
So

00:34:25.080 --> 00:34:28.740
one of our first mistakes was to
try to tackle too many things at once.

00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:31.500
And so part of it is, OK,

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:34.320
this is a really powerful
capability we want to make a

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:37.080
big difference, so let's try to
go as many places as we can.

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:39.360
Now there's some
value to trying to--

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:41.400
if you don't really
know where the value is,

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:44.700
you may kind of try to do
quick proof of concepts in many

00:34:44.700 --> 00:34:47.220
places and whatever one is
successful, then you focus on that.

00:34:49.980 --> 00:34:55.440
But I think we just spent too
much time trying to work on some

00:34:55.440 --> 00:34:58.200
inherited projects that we got
instead of really trying to

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:00.900
focus on where is the
real biggest impact going to be.

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:04.920
It took us about two years
to get to the point where we're

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:08.220
like, OK, we found an area
that can really make a difference.

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:10.200
And then by the fourth year,

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:13.200
that's when things started
to really happen for us.

00:35:13.200 --> 00:35:17.760
And that's when we narrowed
our focus and we honed in,

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:19.800
put all of our efforts on
just a couple of things.

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:21.720
Again, we're a small team,
we can only take on so much.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:25.500
Just focus on one or two things
and just really do well at that.

00:35:28.080 --> 00:35:32.220
And you heard this cliche
about quick wins. Well,

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:33.180
it's a real thing.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:37.800
Showing some progress along
the way-- your senior leaders,

00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:40.500
they want to know that they're
not just wasting their money by

00:35:40.500 --> 00:35:43.200
bringing on an expensive data
science team that's going to

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:46.860
just go off and do
academic exercises,

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:50.520
but are going to actually
make a real difference.

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:54.660
Sorry, still getting over
the whole COVID here.

00:35:56.880 --> 00:35:59.280
And so again, my
advice would be

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:04.320
narrow in, focus on
the highest value areas with

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:06.060
maybe two--

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:08.820
one kind of shorter term quick
win and one kind of longer term

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:10.800
according to the
company priorities.

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:18.780
Hope that helps. Next question?

