00:36:30.320 --> 00:36:31.860
SARANG: Sure.

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:35.220
Thanks for sharing
your experience.

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:36.780
It's been a really
good session.

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:40.860
My question was what approach
do you use to go from like

00:36:41.400 --> 00:36:44.040
proof of concepts to
production ready apps,

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:47.640
especially from security data
governance and scaling perspective.

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:49.020
- Yeah.

00:36:49.860 --> 00:36:54.720
Good, good. So we created
up front a six step process

00:36:55.620 --> 00:36:56.280
where

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:00.960
we kind of went through each
as sort of stage gates to get

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:02.340
all the way to

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:06.180
long term value.

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:09.660
And the first step is
identification/prioritization.

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:16.920
So for our manufacturing
and supply chain leaders,

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:19.380
we had a small
steering committee of leaders and

00:37:19.380 --> 00:37:22.440
analytics leaders that came
together and identified and

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:24.420
prioritize the highest
value use cases.

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:25.920
And that was the first step.

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:29.220
Once they identified those, then
we did the second step,

00:37:29.220 --> 00:37:32.460
which is feasibility where we took
a little bit of time to

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:34.920
gather the data, to look at the

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:36.720
data available,

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:39.840
do we have enough data and
the right kind of data to do a

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:40.620
proof of concept.

00:37:41.340 --> 00:37:42.300
And if so,

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:45.600
then we went to the next
stage which was a proof of concept.

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:46.620
And here's where
we would build--

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:52.380
we're talking the ideal would be
between two and four weeks, quick,

00:37:52.380 --> 00:37:53.640
proof of concept,

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:56.520
using the data that we have
sort of in a sandbox or

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:58.680
offline, we're not building
production ready code,

00:37:58.680 --> 00:37:59.340
we're not building

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:02.460
something that's ready
to go to the live.

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:03.600
We're just trying
to prove the value.

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:07.380
So that's really the idea of
a proof of concept. Two things--

00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:09.780
can we do it and does
it have the value.

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:12.000
Those are the two questions
we're trying to answer with a

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:12.720
proof of concept.

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:15.960
And if the answer to
both of those is yes,

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:19.980
there's real business value and
we're successfully able to do it.

00:38:20.580 --> 00:38:23.580
Then we go to the fourth
stage, which is the pilot.

00:38:23.580 --> 00:38:27.300
And that's where we would
make it more production ready.

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:29.400
Here's where we were going to

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:32.160
partner with our
data engineering team to

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:36.360
take the analytic solution and
put it into production on a

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:38.160
small scale. And think of it as

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:41.160
maybe one manufacturing
site, one line

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:44.100
kind of thing where you're
not scaling it across

00:38:44.940 --> 00:38:46.260
many different places,

00:38:46.260 --> 00:38:50.160
but being more deliberate
in how you're doing it.

00:38:50.160 --> 00:38:51.420
And this is kind of a risk

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:53.040
based approach

00:38:54.060 --> 00:38:55.620
and we were given
permission to fail.

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:57.720
A lot of the proof of
concepts succeeded,

00:38:57.720 --> 00:38:58.620
there were some that did not.

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:03.960
Or some succeeded, but maybe didn't
have as much value as we

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:06.240
anticipated. They were
good, but maybe not as high.

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:08.700
And so focusing on
those highest value

00:39:08.700 --> 00:39:11.580
proof of concepts going on
to the next stage of piloting.

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:15.120
And then those pilots that
are successful, for example,

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:19.980
what I talked about the
monoclonal antibody is very

00:39:19.980 --> 00:39:24.780
successful. We want to scale
that to other sites, other products,

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:27.720
other stages of the process

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:29.760
and

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:33.240
leverage what we learn there
and scale it across other areas.

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:36.300
And so that's that
scale up stage,

00:39:36.300 --> 00:39:40.740
which would be after stage
five, the scale up after piloting.

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:43.680
Then the last stage is once
we scale that up, it's sustaining.

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:45.600
So how do we sustain
the value long term.

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:51.360
And so those six stages are
how we thought about it upfront.

00:39:51.360 --> 00:39:54.960
And as a team, we

00:39:54.960 --> 00:39:56.460
had the team size,
but also some

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:58.680
resources for partner support.

00:39:58.680 --> 00:40:03.600
So we had also some consulting
support to help us scale faster.

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:06.300
And within the first year or so

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:08.460
of us getting started,

00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:14.460
