00:47:14.060 --> 00:47:15.060
-

00:47:15.060 --> 00:47:19.020
The most upvoted question
on Slido is how do you

00:47:19.020 --> 00:47:19.440
decide/design

00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:24.060
requirements for DS, which
I'm assuming is data science,

00:47:24.060 --> 00:47:26.640
versus DA, which I'm
assuming is data analysis,

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:29.060
other than their
academic background.

00:47:29.060 --> 00:47:31.380
- Great question. So

00:47:33.480 --> 00:47:37.680
we have created just last
year and launched what we

00:47:37.680 --> 00:47:40.620
have is the career

00:47:40.620 --> 00:47:46.320
framework, career progression framework.
And this came out

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:47.640
after I had designed the org.

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:49.920
So let me kind of give both
of those and how they come together.

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:51.480
So when I first
designed the org,

00:47:51.480 --> 00:47:56.100
the idea was that we wanted
to hire people that can be really

00:47:56.100 --> 00:47:58.860
good at manipulating data,
that can create visuals,

00:47:58.860 --> 00:48:01.740
create dashboards, that can
communicate well with the business,

00:48:01.740 --> 00:48:05.640
but don't necessarily have an
advanced degree in data science. So

00:48:06.540 --> 00:48:07.680
analytics is a spectrum.

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:09.720
There's this whole

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:12.600
spectrum continuum
between someone who,

00:48:13.980 --> 00:48:15.240
on one end, is

00:48:16.740 --> 00:48:20.940
competent and familiar with
data analytics tools and capabilities

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:23.280
but not

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:26.400
necessarily you don't
have years of math

00:48:26.400 --> 00:48:29.700
background and all the
math that's underneath it whereas

00:48:29.700 --> 00:48:32.520
someone who has an advanced
degree in analytics and

00:48:32.520 --> 00:48:34.980
multiple years of
grad school, has

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:38.040
all the mathematical
foundations behind and really

00:48:38.040 --> 00:48:40.800
knows that very well, and
there's all the space in between.

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:44.640
And so we felt like there was
a lot of value in having both

00:48:44.640 --> 00:48:45.600
ends of that spectrum.

00:48:45.600 --> 00:48:46.860
And so

00:48:48.120 --> 00:48:51.420
the design was to have
some data analysts

00:48:51.420 --> 00:48:53.040
who have

00:48:54.420 --> 00:48:55.020
bachelor's degrees.

00:48:56.220 --> 00:48:59.100
And so the answer to the
question is probably the degree

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:02.460
being counted the most strong
indicator of what a data

00:49:02.460 --> 00:49:03.180
analyst would be.

00:49:03.780 --> 00:49:04.620
But basically,

00:49:04.620 --> 00:49:09.060
these are people that don't
want to necessarily build models.

00:49:09.060 --> 00:49:12.060
They're interested
in data engineering,

00:49:12.060 --> 00:49:13.380
they're interested
in data visuals,

00:49:13.380 --> 00:49:16.620
they're interested in solving those
problems kind of warm a

00:49:16.620 --> 00:49:19.440
descriptive way and
aren't as interested in

00:49:20.400 --> 00:49:21.540
machine learning or AI.

00:49:22.200 --> 00:49:23.460
We have people
that are like that.

00:49:23.460 --> 00:49:26.340
And those are our
key data analysts.

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:27.600
And they're a smaller group.

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:30.120
We have a much bigger
group of data scientists.

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:33.720
And data scientists are those
that have those advanced degrees

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:35.520
in

00:49:35.520 --> 00:49:38.280
science, statistics, et cetera.

00:49:38.280 --> 00:49:41.640
And then they really want
to focus on building the models

00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:46.500
and refine those models, MLOps, all
those kinds of processes. And so

00:49:46.500 --> 00:49:47.820
we have both those.

00:49:47.820 --> 00:49:50.640
Now in late last year,

00:49:50.640 --> 00:49:54.180
all of ZTD or Zoetis Tech
and Digital rolled out a career

00:49:54.180 --> 00:49:55.440
progression framework

00:49:55.440 --> 00:49:59.640
where we connected those
together. And so data analysts

00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:01.320
that wanted to

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:04.260
progress to be a data
scientist would have that opportunity.

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:07.560
And it may mean that they
have many more years of experience

00:50:07.560 --> 00:50:10.800
or may mean they get a master's
degree along the way. And

00:50:11.460 --> 00:50:14.400
we're a company that
supports our team members in

00:50:15.480 --> 00:50:17.040
furthering their education.

00:50:17.040 --> 00:50:22.140
And so we would help them
along the way to get further education.

00:50:22.140 --> 00:50:25.200
And so they would have an
opportunity then progress and

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:26.580
to go higher levels

00:50:27.300 --> 00:50:29.940
in the company in terms of
being an individual contributor

00:50:29.940 --> 00:50:30.720
within data science.

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:34.440
So I hope that
answered the question.

00:50:34.440 --> 00:50:36.840
We both are highly
valued and needed,

00:50:36.840 --> 00:50:39.840
used for different parts
of the whole puzzle.

